    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 555,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70197,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 125,
        "headingId": 555,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>No characteristic findings are known.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:33:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 510,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 549,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70198,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 125,
        "headingId": 549,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>PSTT consists of mononucleated implantation-type intermediate trophoblastic cells, as well as infiltrative single or loosely cohesive cells with abundant eosinophilic or focally vacuolated cytoplasm, with irregular nuclei, often appearing smudged. PSTT distinctively invades into the muscular walls of vessels, with partial or complete replacement usually associated with fibrinoid change. PSTT expresses HSD3B1, inhibin, GATA3, and CK18, and it is specifically positive for hPL {26457351}. Inhibin reactivity is patchy, while hCG is focally positive. p63 is negative in PSTT {17498600; 26457351}, and this is helpful in the differentiation from epithelioid trophoblastic tumour, in which p63 is positive. The Ki-67 index ranges between 10% and 50%.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:33:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
